-<p>Revision 8 read the 2-edge shell as 2 marginals with their pairing lost. Theorem 25 below shows that is not the right description, since the imbalance marginal is already determined by shell 1. The census agrees. Of the classes colliding through shell 2 on 17 vertices, only 78 of 820 have different triple multisets \(\{(b,\{a,c\})\}\). The main obstruction is that pair-level data itself is incomplete: nonisomorphic trees with equal triple multisets first occur at 10 vertices, and there are 742 such classes at 17 vertices. Every one of those classes through 17 vertices is split by shell 3. So incidence information begins at 3-edge contractions. For example, the 10-vertex pair has equal triples and differs in shell 3 by \(-1,+2,-1\) at volumes 1, 2, 3 and \(+1,-2,+1\) at volumes 11, 12, 13.</p>
