 <p><span class="lab">Proof.</span> The differences give \(P\bar P\), so \((P-\bar P)^2=(P+\bar P)^2-4P\bar P\) is known, and \(\mathbb Z[x]\) is an integral domain, so \(P-\bar P\) is determined up to sign.</p>
 <p>So on a weighted path, the contraction level, shells 1 and 2 are already complete, and homometry is no obstacle. In a real caterpillar the pendant edges add to \(B\) the term \(\sum_i(L_ix^{a_i-1}+R_ix^{n-a_i-1})\) with \(L_i\), \(R_i\) the pendants on each side of cut \(i\), and that contaminates the difference histogram.</p>
 <div class="th"><p><span class="lab">Theorem 28 (shell 2 is not complete), proved.</span> For every \(s\ge1\), the caterpillars with pendant sequences \((s,s+1,s-1,2s+1,s+2)\) and \((s,2s+1,s+1,s-1,s+2)\) on \(n=6s+8\) vertices are non-isomorphic and have the same \(A\) and \(B\), and therefore equal shells 1 and 2. They also have equal degree histograms.</p></div>
-<p><span class="lab">Proof.</span> All exponents and multiplicities are polynomial in \(s\), and the 2 expressions agree identically as a symbolic computation; non-isomorphism is visible from the 2 reversal classes. <span class="lab">Exact check:</span> at \(s=1,2,3\) the pairs first differ in shell 3. Such collisions are rare: through 26 vertices at most 1392 of 4,196,351 caterpillars collide, and every collision class checked at the 5 orders tested, through 20 vertices, is split by shell 3.</p>
+<p><span class="lab">Proof.</span> All exponents and multiplicities are polynomial in \(s\), and the 2 expressions agree identically as a symbolic computation; non-isomorphism follows from the canonical nonleaf spine, which is a 5-vertex path even at s=1. A forward match fails at the second entry, s+1 versus 2s+1; a reversed match fails at the first entry, s versus s+2. Both inequalities hold for every s greater than or equal to 1. <span class="lab">Exact check:</span> at \(s=1,2,3\) the pairs first differ in shell 3. At n=26 alone, the reported census leaves 1392 excess caterpillars among 4,196,351 tested; this is neither a cumulative count through n=26 nor a count of all graphs in collision classes. Shell 3 splits every collision class at the five explicitly tested orders 11, 14, 17, 18 and 20. This is finite evidence, not an all-order assertion.</p>
 <p><span class="lab">Hunt.</span> No collision has been found. All trees on 19 to 22 vertices, about 8.9 million, were tested with a staged filter, and the counts 317955, 823065, 2144505 and 5623756 match the known numbers of trees. The joint low-order data separate them all: after shell 1, the degree histogram and the edge degree-sum histogram, 2,351,536 excess trees remain at 22 vertices; after volume 4, 46,912; after shells through 3, none. Every separation is rigorous. Low volume and low energy together are far stronger than either alone, so whether a fixed bidegree suffices for all \(n\) is a precise question, though I expect not.</p>
 
 <p>This also corrects the earlier reading of the rank tables. They are exact and remain useful, but the rank of volume slices is a shadow of this lattice sum. Revision 6 also suggested that poles at \(q=\infty\) might be artifacts of a formal expansion. That was wrong, as the outside supplement observes: at fixed volume the maximum energy is \(3V^2\), attained by single towers, so this end is a genuine extremal problem. It has its own exact limit.</p>
